vanadis: return ENOSYS from riscv_hwprobe

Vanadis returned success from riscv_hwprobe (syscall 258), but it did not
write the values of the key/value pairs.  Newer glibc versions, such as
the one of nixpkgs, select their RISC-V string functions with ifuncs.  The resolvers set only the key
of the pair and then read its value, so with this success they read
uninitialized stack memory as the features of the CPU.  When bit 2
(RISCV_HWPROBE_IMA_V) happened to be set, glibc selected the vector (RVV)
functions, for example __strrchr_vector.  Vanadis cannot decode RVV
instructions, so the program stopped with a DECODE_FAULT.  The balar
Vanadis test (vecadd, static glibc) stopped in this way.

Return ENOSYS, as Linux before 6.4 (which has no riscv_hwprobe) does.
glibc then uses AT_HWCAP, which Vanadis gives as 0, and selects the
generic functions.

--- a/src/sst/elements/vanadis/os/vriscvcpuos.h
+++ b/src/sst/elements/vanadis/os/vriscvcpuos.h
@@ -323,9 +323,12 @@
     VanadisSyscallEvent* HWPROBE( int hw_thr ) {
         #ifdef VANADIS_BUILD_DEBUG
         // Warning but ifdef'd because this rarely causes a problem
-        output_->output("Warning: VANADIS_SYSCALL_RISCV64_HWPROBE not implemented return success\n");
+        output_->output("Warning: VANADIS_SYSCALL_RISCV64_HWPROBE not implemented return ENOSYS\n");
         #endif
-        recvSyscallResp(new VanadisSyscallResponse(0));
+        // Return ENOSYS, as Linux before 6.4 does.  Success without the
+        // values makes glibc read its uninitialized values as the features
+        // of the CPU, and then its ifuncs can select vector (RVV) functions.
+        recvSyscallResp(new VanadisSyscallResponse(-ENOSYS));
         return nullptr;
     }
 
